# S5 — lower is better (lara-syntax@0.4)
#
# The fourth strict-certificate worked example, and the third on ord@1. S2 and
# S3 compare accuracy, where a larger number wins. S5 compares PERPLEXITY, where
# a smaller one does — and it is written with the same `relation =
# strictly-better` an author writes in S2.
#
# That is the point of the example. "sys_new outperforms sys_base" is one
# research relation, and an author should state it once, the same way, whether
# the metric runs up or down. What differs is not the claim but the arithmetic
# that supports it, and the arithmetic is selected by a fact about the metric:
# `measurand perplexity : Num where lower-is-better`, declared in
# ord-ppl-v1.policy.lara. Under that polarity the (strictly-better,
# lower-is-better) scheme selects rules whose strict conclusion is
# num_lt(Sv, Bv) — ours below theirs — so the goal generated here is
# num_lt(28.4, 31.6), the mirror image of S2's num_lt(0.71, 0.74).
#
# Why this cannot be inferred, and so must be declared. Given only this
# artifact — two reported cells and an attestation that they are comparable —
# nothing recovers the fact that lower perplexity is the better result. The
# numbers do not say it, the predicate does not say it, and the way the numbers
# are used does not say it. It is knowledge about perplexity, and the surface
# asks for it in exactly one place.
#
# Why the elaborator cannot get it wrong. Direction is not a flag the elaborator
# applies to an argument order; it lives in the policy's rules, welded to the
# bridge's conclusion through shared variables. Selecting a scheme selects the
# goal shape, the rules that consume it, and the comparative claim they license,
# all at once. There is no state in which the generated goal says "ours is
# lower" while the bridge concludes on the strength of "ours is higher" — the
# two are the same variables.
#
# The strict step still refuses everything it refused in S2. ord@1 re-checks the
# generated certificate in exact rational arithmetic against the generated goal:
# had the two cells been the other way around, 31.6 < 28.4 is false and this
# artifact would be an R13 replay rejection, polarity declaration or not.
# Declaring a polarity chooses which comparison to make; it never decides one.
#
# WHAT CHANGED AT @0.4. Six `let` bindings now name the two systems, metric,
# dataset, and reported scores once. Every ground-term use below refers to those
# names, and c1's prose interpolates the same four system/score values, so the
# expanded text is byte-for-byte unchanged. The typed `on perplexity @
# wikitext103` selector remains explicit and unchanged: it selects the @0.3
# comparison scheme rather than acting as a term-substitution site.
#
# The @0.3 comparison expansion, lower-is-better policy choice, strict
# certificate, scientific claim, and expected verdict are unchanged.
#
# Expected verdict is at the bottom of this file (golden oracle).

artifact ord_ppl_demo at sha256:5555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555
policy ord-ppl-v1
use backends [ord@1]

let candidate = sys_new
let baseline = sys_base
let metric = perplexity
let dataset = wikitext103
let candidate_score = 28.4
let baseline_score = 31.6

# --- The comparative claim the bridge rule delivers -------------------------------------------
# Same predicate as S2's: `better`. A reader comparing the two examples sees one claim shape and
# two metrics, which is the reading the surface is for.

claim c1
  nl      = "{candidate} outperforms {baseline} on WikiText-103 perplexity ({candidate_score} vs {baseline_score})"
  formal  = better(candidate, baseline, metric, dataset)
  binding = { author = alice, rationale = "Ordered comparison of two reported perplexity cells; lower is better.", audit-status = reviewed }

# --- The two measured cells the certificate consults ------------------------------------------
# Nothing here marks a direction. Both leaves are ordinary reported cells, and swapping which
# system is which would produce an equally well-formed pair of leaves — which is precisely why
# the policy has to declare the polarity.

leaf e1 : reports(exp1, score_cell(baseline, metric, dataset, baseline_score))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [evidence/tables/perplexity.md#row=sys_base]

leaf e2 : reports(exp1, score_cell(candidate, metric, dataset, candidate_score))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [evidence/tables/perplexity.md#row=sys_new]

# The binding, exactly as in S2: which systems and measurand the two numbers belong to. Its
# argument order does not mirror with the polarity — the system's value comes first here as it
# does in S2 — so one attestation vocabulary serves both directions.

leaf e3 : comparison_setup(candidate, baseline, metric, dataset, candidate_score, baseline_score)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [evidence/tables/perplexity.md#caption]

# --- The comparison: the same source shape as S2, mirrored by declaration ----------------------
# Read this block beside examples/S2/example.lara. Every field means the same thing and
# `relation` reads the same. `result = e2` still names the leaf carrying our system's number and
# `baseline = e1` still names the baseline's; the author does not restate the direction, because
# the author already declared it once, about the metric.
#
# What the elaborator generates from it is mirrored: the goal num_lt(28.4, 31.6) rather than
# num_lt(31.6, 28.4), and the certificate slots to match — (prem 0) is the system cell here,
# where in S2 it is the baseline cell. None of that is written by hand in either file.
# The sub-claim likewise derives both quoted scores from the exact evidence leaves consumed by
# the generated arithmetic rather than from parallel prose constants.

comparison : better(candidate, baseline) on perplexity @ wikitext103
  relation = strictly-better
  recheck  = a1
  bridge   = a2
  result   = e2
  baseline = e1
  binding  = e3
  claims c2
    nl      = "The reported system perplexity {cell e2} is strictly below the reported baseline perplexity {cell e1}"
    binding = { author = alice, audit-status = reviewed }
  supports c1

status c1
status c2

# ==============================================================================================
# EXPECTED VERDICT (golden oracle)
# ==============================================================================================
# Compiled AF: a1 (the strict ord@1 comparison step, replaying num_lt(28.4, 31.6)) and a2 (the
# defeasible bridge, with a1 as a sub-argument), both unattacked -> in (grounded).
# Claim status:
#   c1 -> justified (a2 is a complete argument for it, with no attackers)
#   c2 -> justified (a1 is in; the mirrored arithmetic replays and accepts)
